WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.100
Ask anyone that has been through it, and they’ll
all tell you the same: that moving home is

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.430
maybe one of the most stressful things a normal
person can ever experience.

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:12.080
It’s a logistical nightmare right from the
start.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.950
The moment you talk to a realtor about being
interested in selling your current house and

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:19.330
buying another, everything goes downhill from
there.

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:23.449
After that moment, an avalanche of things
comes hurtling towards you – finding a place

00:00:23.449 --> 00:00:28.089
you like, making an offer, letting people
look around your house, waiting for them to

00:00:28.089 --> 00:00:32.430
make a counteroffer, exchanging contracts
– and that’s before you have even thought

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:33.430
about packing.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:38.230
And, as Milo had learned, doing all of that
on your own only makes the stress of moving

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:40.170
feel all the more potent.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.870
But he had finally made it.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:47.370
After a constant back and forth with his realtor,
the time had come for him to pack up all his

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:51.550
worldly possessions and relocate to a brand-new
place to call home.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:56.580
He had felt it was long overdue for him to
get a change of scenery, and luckily just

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.300
the right place had come onto the market to
answer that call.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:05.840
It was a pretty big house, bigger than Milo’s
previous home, but considerably cheaper – in

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.740
fact, he thought it had been significantly
undervalued.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:14.590
The house had an almost Victorian era feel
to it, all beautifully carved and varnished

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:20.520
woodwork and creaky old floorboards but in
more of an elegant, refined sort of way, rather

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.189
than a creepier one.

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:25.600
That’s not to say Milo’s new place wasn’t
without its more…

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.460
unsettling elements.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:32.030
Aside from being big, spacious, and easy to
imagine as being haunted, the creepiest thing

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:37.280
about the house – aside from its frighteningly
low price – was the story of what happened

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.920
to the previous owners.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.700
“Obviously you know that, legally, we have
to disclose whether or not anyone died on

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:47.499
this property,” the realtor had explained,
wearing a forced grin as she showed Milo around

00:01:47.499 --> 00:01:48.499
the house.

00:01:48.499 --> 00:01:53.020
Hearing that sentence, he could feel the hairs
on the back of his neck stand up.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:56.020
“Someone died here?” he asked in disbelief.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.060
“No, no, of course not, sir!”

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:04.159
the realtor instantly walked back her statement,
less in an effort to ease her client’s nerves,

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:08.280
but more to ensure that she still closed the
deal and earned her commission bonus.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.450
“Then why the hell would you start a sentence
like that?”

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:12.470
Milo asked.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:17.470
“We just have to give full disclosure, but
I assure you, the previous residents didn’t

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.290
die on the grounds of this house.”

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:23.930
She gave that faked smile again; it was pretty
ineffectual at reassuring him.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:26.540
“So, what happened to the last owner?”

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.830
“Well, it’s a bit of a mystery, to be
honest, a local legend, if you like.

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:35.290
But hey, that gives the place more flavor,
having some spooky history, right?

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:37.450
Think of what a conversation starter that
will be!”

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:41.879
The realtor ended her sentence with a forced,
snorting laugh, but reigned it back in when

00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:45.030
she realized Milo wasn’t laughing with her.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:49.470
“As far as I know, there used to be an older
couple living here, the Shaws.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:53.360
They were apparently very quiet, reclusive,
kept to themselves a lot; I’m sure you know

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:54.360
the type.”

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.830
Milo couldn’t help but notice she shot him
a look as she said that part, as if she was

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:03.260
silently passing judgement on the fact that
he was planning on moving in alone.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:07.560
“But they used to spend a lot of time tending
the garden out front, so their neighbors would

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:12.580
see them pretty frequently, which meant they
were all okay and no one had any bad mishaps

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:13.580
in the house.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.300
You know how old folks can be, such a worry
when they get to that age-”

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:21.019
“Yeah, totally,” Milo interjected, aware
the realtor was stalling.

00:03:21.019 --> 00:03:25.480
He repeated: “So, what actually happened
to them?”

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:28.450
“Well, that’s the thing,” she shrugged.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:30.600
“Nobody knows the full story.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.920
One day the neighbors across the street noticed
they hadn’t seen Mr or Mrs. Shaw for quite

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:34.920
a while.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.989
So, naturally, they came right on over here
to see if they were in.

00:03:37.989 --> 00:03:41.379
The last thing they wanted was to assume everything
was hunky-dory here.

00:03:41.379 --> 00:03:43.420
I mean, better safe than sorry, right?

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:47.890
But once they got inside, they couldn’t
find either of them, Mr. and Mrs. Shaw were

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:48.890
just… gone.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:50.430
Vanished without a trace.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.469
Most people on the street thought that they
had sold the place, kept it quiet and moved

00:03:53.469 --> 00:03:56.329
away, retired to somewhere nice and exotic.”

00:03:56.329 --> 00:03:58.159
“Oh, well, that sounds…

00:03:58.159 --> 00:04:00.150
nice, I suppose?”

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:04.200
Milo replied, relieved to hear there hadn’t
been any brutal unsolved murders in the house,

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.090
and that it most likely wasn’t haunted.

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:10.129
“That’s just what the neighbors think
anyway,” the realtor continued.

00:04:10.129 --> 00:04:12.470
“Of course, they hadn’t sold the house.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:13.690
They’d just left it.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:17.889
When my firm came in to repossess this place,
it had all their belongings in it.

00:04:17.889 --> 00:04:19.660
Some might even still be laying around here.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:22.270
Anyway, did you want to have a look at this
contract?”

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:27.060
While, strictly speaking, Milo was the only
human living in his new home, he was never

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.520
fully alone.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:32.990
He had arrived with his two closest friends,
a pair of pets he had adopted while living

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:34.490
in his old house.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:39.560
One was a hamster by the name of Donut, named
after his round little body and the golden-brown

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:44.180
shade of his fur had reminded Milo of the
glazed, sugary baked treat.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:48.860
The other was Pixel, a bearded dragon with
a pattern on his scales that resembled some

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:51.110
kind of mosaic art.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:55.949
The first order of business, Milo had decided,
was before even unpacking, he needed to make

00:04:55.949 --> 00:05:01.220
sure his scaly and furry friends each had
a suitable place to stay and that he got them

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:02.220
fed.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:06.810
Little did he realize there was something
else living in the house with them, something

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:12.270
that was far bigger and was getting much hungrier
than either of his pets would.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:16.680
Looking around the house, Milo stumbled across
a few boxes that he hadn’t brought with

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:17.680
him.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:21.550
Like the realtor had said, the Shaws still
had some of their possessions left lying around

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:22.680
the place.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:27.360
Most of it was useless to Milo, an old man’s
clothes or a set of knitting needles and rolls

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.819
of colorful thread.

00:05:28.819 --> 00:05:32.990
It was those particular clues that seemed
to imply Mrs. Shaw had a lot of free time

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:37.169
on her hands and maybe took up knitting as
a hobby during retirement.

00:05:37.169 --> 00:05:42.130
Of course, the bigger clue was the huge, handmade
blanket that Milo found draped over some of

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:44.550
the remaining boxes in the attic.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:48.940
He wasn’t exactly well versed in knitwear,
but he could appreciate the craft behind this

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:49.940
soft blanket.

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:54.720
It had clearly had a lot of time and effort
put into making it, painstakingly knitting

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.830
each and every individual thread, looping
it around a pair of steel needles, and eventually

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:04.940
– after hours of wearing out shaking, bony
fingers – producing something that actually

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:06.910
looked quite nice.

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:09.830
Even though the idea of sitting and knitting
a blanket might have been one of the most

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:15.020
boring uses of time Milo could imagine, he
still had to admit he was surprised that the

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.599
Shaws had left this particular piece of bedding
behind.

00:06:18.599 --> 00:06:22.720
It seemed like it would be pretty comfy to
sleep under, and might help keep a person

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:25.960
warm at night now that the colder seasons
were approaching.

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:31.139
What’s more, was that the blanket was clean,
almost like it had been freshly washed.

00:06:31.139 --> 00:06:37.130
There was no old person smell on it, no dirt
or discoloration, not a stain, and it hadn’t

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:39.610
even accumulated any dust in its fibers.

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:44.000
In fact, he noticed that there was hardly
any dust at all up in the attic; despite the

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.259
house being empty for so long.

00:06:46.259 --> 00:06:50.099
He assumed the realtors had hired someone
as a caretaker while they found a buyer for

00:06:50.099 --> 00:06:53.259
the place, and they had kept the place clean.

00:06:53.259 --> 00:06:56.260
After all, what other conclusion could he
have possibly drawn?

00:06:56.260 --> 00:07:00.490
It’s not as if something had eaten all the
dust, that would be absurd…

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:05.470
He’d have a sort through of the Shaws leftovers
later, maybe sell some of it off at a local

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:08.129
yard sale or give it to a thrift store.

00:07:08.129 --> 00:07:13.379
But that blanket might come in handy when
it started snowing, so Milo had half a mind

00:07:13.379 --> 00:07:14.419
to keep it…

00:07:14.419 --> 00:07:18.569
Now, part of what makes moving home such a
stressful life experience isn’t just all

00:07:18.569 --> 00:07:21.509
the logistical and administrative parts of
the process.

00:07:21.509 --> 00:07:26.840
It’s only made worse by the fact that it
takes forever to move into a new house fully.

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:31.600
That’s the part everyone always forgets
about, the months after a move where you’re

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:37.410
forced to live among towers of cardboard boxes,
all your worldly possessions buried deep within

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:42.270
them, and you can never remember which crate
anything is in when you urgently need it.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:46.970
So, realizing it would take him much longer
to unpack all of his things, Milo set about

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:52.100
making sure he had everything that Donut and
Pixel would need already to hand.

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:56.750
The latter of his two pets, the bearded dragon,
usually spent most of his days in a spacious

00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:58.099
glass tank.

00:07:58.099 --> 00:08:02.660
Milo didn’t love the idea of keeping either
of his two best buds so confined, but Pixel

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:07.639
never went very far anyway, content to lay
under a heat lamp almost perfectly still for

00:08:07.639 --> 00:08:08.800
most of the day.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:12.169
Donut, on the other hand, was much more of
a free spirit.

00:08:12.169 --> 00:08:16.939
The tiny brown hamster physically could not
stay still, and most days seemed to be filled

00:08:16.939 --> 00:08:19.180
with more energy than Milo was.

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:24.410
As a result, he let the little guy roll about
inside a little plastic ball all day long,

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:26.669
until his little hamster feet gave out.

00:08:26.669 --> 00:08:32.180
And now, Donut had much more space to zip
around inside his ball, so while he entertained

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:37.370
himself, Milo could focus on what needed fixing,
cleaning, and generally improving around the

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:38.370
house.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:42.169
It didn’t take him long to realize, however,
that the realtors had pulled a bit of a fast

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:43.469
one on him.

00:08:43.469 --> 00:08:47.500
Despite the absolute steal of a price that
Milo had been offered, it quickly became clear

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:51.610
that the unknown fate of the previous residents
wasn’t the only detail about the house that

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:54.089
had been conveniently kept hidden.

00:08:54.089 --> 00:08:58.580
The place was in dire need of repair, with
a lot of the old woodwork rapidly rotting

00:08:58.580 --> 00:08:59.580
away.

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.790
If left unchecked, parts of the house could
collapse and come apart at the seams.

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:09.120
To make matters worse, the boiler was an ancient,
iron monstrosity that was barely able to produce

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:13.790
warm water, which would quickly become an
even bigger problem when the weather started

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:14.830
to get colder.

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:19.430
Then, to top off the trifecta of unforeseen
issues and teething problems with his new

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:21.600
place, Milo couldn’t sleep.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.750
It was to be expected.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:27.589
After all, the house was old, worn out, and
had definitely seen better days.

00:09:27.589 --> 00:09:32.190
There were bound to be a few noises; the odd
creak coming from upstairs when Milo and his

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:36.540
two pets were all downstairs, the tapping
of branches against windows blowing in the

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:37.980
wind outside.

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:42.339
But for some reason, every audible disturbance
that emanated from some hidden corner of the

00:09:42.339 --> 00:09:47.760
building only seemed to get a thousand times
louder when the sun went down.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:52.209
At night time, every squeaky floorboard or
random noise of the house settling was nearly

00:09:52.209 --> 00:09:57.070
deafening, enough to pull Milo right out of
what little restless sleep he was getting.

00:09:57.070 --> 00:10:01.810
The worst part about it was – though he
couldn’t help it – it made him feel unwelcome,

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:06.170
as if Mr. and Mrs. Shaw were angered that
he had moved in.

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:11.529
In the dark, it was hard not to picture every
sound as one or both of the old couple creeping

00:10:11.529 --> 00:10:17.420
through the corridors, coming to reclaim their
home and ‘remove’ Milo from the premises.

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:21.820
One night, the noises started invading what
little sleep Milo did manage to get, spilling

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:25.410
into his dreams and causing them to devolve
into unsettling nightmares.

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:29.839
There was an old photograph in a frame that
had come across in the personal effects lefts

00:10:29.839 --> 00:10:35.380
behind by the Shaws, showing the pair of them
staring disapprovingly out of the image at

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:36.380
him.

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:40.410
Now, thanks to the creaking wooden structure
of the house and the sounds it made at night,

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:44.709
Milo was seeing the old couple in every bad
dream he had.

00:10:44.709 --> 00:10:49.970
Both their faces were locked in those same
still, frozen expressions of contempt, as

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:54.570
they tried to exorcise him like an interloper
on their property.

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:58.529
That was the final nail in the coffin that
made Milo realize he needed to look at other

00:10:58.529 --> 00:10:59.529
options.

00:10:59.529 --> 00:11:04.060
Surely, there had to be some way to induce
a deep enough sleep so that the sounds the

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:08.529
house was making weren’t keeping him up
or incepting nightmares anymore.

00:11:08.529 --> 00:11:12.399
He called the local doctor, arranging a consultation
for later that day.

00:11:12.399 --> 00:11:15.639
As he hung up the phone, something nudged
against his foot.

00:11:15.639 --> 00:11:20.850
It was Donut, having rolled through the maze
of cardboard boxes still filling the house.

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:25.220
Milo took one look at the little brown-furred
hamster, then the crates that still littered

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:26.329
the place.

00:11:26.329 --> 00:11:30.020
Grabbing the leftover items that the Shaws
had forgotten to take with them before they

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:33.370
vanished, Milo moved those boxes out into
the shed.

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:37.270
He knew he was probably reaching, that it
was hardly likely to make even the slightest

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:42.160
difference to his sleeping, but maybe keeping
their stuff away from him would keep the old

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:44.000
couple out of his head.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:48.230
The only thing he kept in the house was the
blanket; there was no use letting it go to

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:53.040
waste, especially knowing that the boiler
was on the blink and it would be cold soon.

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:58.310
He rolled it up and left it on his bed, leaving
his more mobile pet in the same room before

00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:00.450
heading out to see the doctor.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:04.790
When Milo got back, a bottle of prescription
sleeping pills in his pocket, he couldn’t

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:07.200
help but notice his room seemed different.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:10.590
The blanket looked like it had fallen off
the bed and onto the floor, for one.

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:16.290
But more worryingly, Donut’s plastic travel
ball had been split open, laying in pieces

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:20.920
on the ground… with its furry little occupant
nowhere to be seen.

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:25.780
Over the next few hours, Milo searched every
corner of the house, calling out to his hamster,

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:28.580
trying to lure it back with morsels of food.

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:33.290
But Donut didn’t seem to be anywhere, he
wasn’t even underneath the knitted blanket.

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:35.240
Still, there was no sign.

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:39.510
As the worry set in, Milo hurriedly checked
Pixel’s cage to see if he was gone too,

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:45.410
but the lizard was relaxing, as lethargic
as ever, completely unphased by what was going

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:46.410
on.

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:50.090
It certainly seemed that, when it rained,
it really poured in his new house.

00:12:50.090 --> 00:12:55.220
Just as he was searching for his missing hamster,
Milo heard a new, horrible sound echo through

00:12:55.220 --> 00:12:56.579
the corridors.

00:12:56.579 --> 00:13:01.010
This one wasn’t so much a scary noise, even
if it did make him jump, but it was more the

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:03.270
inconvenience that came with it.

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:08.209
Finally, giving out after god knows how many
years it had been installed for, the house’s

00:13:08.209 --> 00:13:10.300
boiler burst.

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:14.550
Spending the afternoon into the evening failing
to track down one of his missing pets and

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:19.699
stopping the huge iron cast boiler from flooding
the basement wasn’t exactly what Milo had

00:13:19.699 --> 00:13:23.250
in mind for fun activities to do when he got
home.

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:27.720
Going to bed frustrated made it as hard to
sleep as all the nightly cacophony of creaking

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:30.810
floorboards and branches raking against the
windows.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:34.910
He tapped out two of the pills the doctor
had prescribed him, knocking them back with

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:40.520
a sharp motion of his head and a swig of cold
water, before laying back in bed.

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:45.389
As night had fallen, the temperature had also
plummeted too, and not having a boiler to

00:13:45.389 --> 00:13:49.850
heat his room meant that Milo couldn’t stop
himself from shivering in the cold, his breath

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:52.269
forming clouds in front of his face.

00:13:52.269 --> 00:13:56.760
He reached for the knitted blanket and threw
it over himself, curling up underneath it

00:13:56.760 --> 00:14:01.790
to try and provide an extra layer of warmth
to protect himself from the gnawing cold.

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:06.600
The pills did as they were meant to, helping
Milo to quickly slip into a much deeper sleep

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:08.790
than he had experienced in a while.

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:11.390
Although, it didn’t seem to help with the
nightmares.

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:13.779
Mr. and Mrs. Shaw came back again.

00:14:13.779 --> 00:14:16.639
This time they had Milo’s arms and legs
tied up.

00:14:16.639 --> 00:14:20.650
With their matching, disapproving faces, the
elderly couple threw something over their

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:24.519
helpless victim and tried to smother him to
death.

00:14:24.519 --> 00:14:28.339
For a dream, it felt so intense, so real.

00:14:28.339 --> 00:14:31.339
Milo could feel a heavy weight on top of his
body as he slept.

00:14:31.339 --> 00:14:35.610
In fact, it felt like it was all around him,
engulfing and crushing the life out of him

00:14:35.610 --> 00:14:36.610
from all sides.

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:41.589
But it was the feeling of something wet against
his arm, the sensation of liquid against his

00:14:41.589 --> 00:14:47.199
skin, and numbness where his hand should be
were what finally pulled him out of the dream…

00:14:47.199 --> 00:14:48.670
and into the nightmare.

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:54.410
Through the dark and his cloudy vision, Milo
could see a wide, gaping maw filled with teeth.

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:58.570
The feeling of being crushed was still surrounding
him, and despite kicking his legs, and trying

00:14:58.570 --> 00:15:02.680
to free himself from the confinement, it didn’t
stop squeezing him tighter.

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:06.480
His panic was already making it harder to
catch his breath, but now he could barely

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:11.350
fill his lungs enough to scream for help…
not that anyone was around to hear him.

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:13.970
But it was the sight of his arm that chilled
Milo’s blood.

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:15.970
It wasn’t there anymore.

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:19.230
Part of it just above the elbow was just…
gone.

00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:24.800
His hand, fingers, every internal bone, and
muscle had been reduced to a bloody mess,

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:28.019
a slurry of red, melting and coming apart.

00:15:28.019 --> 00:15:32.920
It was runny, little more than liquid, resembling
the consistency of sand when it’s underwater

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:34.100
and becomes sludge.

00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:39.480
More of his flesh was liquefied, going further
up his arm as the blanket pulled its prey

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:44.320
into its mouth until Milo was no more, not
a trace of him left…

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:46.339
just like the previous owners.

00:15:46.339 --> 00:15:50.589
Little did Milo – or the Shaws and Donut
before him – know, but he’d been the victim

00:15:50.589 --> 00:15:54.630
of a creature that was part of a rather unique
species.

00:15:54.630 --> 00:15:58.389
While they will often vary in their size,
shape, pattern, and other aspects of their

00:15:58.389 --> 00:16:04.819
appearance, SCP-799 always seem to be ordinary
items of knitted bedwear, at least at first.

00:16:04.819 --> 00:16:10.070
They retain heat like a normal blanket would,
are soft to the touch, and, for the most part,

00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:11.990
don’t seem directly harmful.

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:14.519
And usually, they aren’t.

00:16:14.519 --> 00:16:20.670
SCP-799s are incapable of much movement, instead
laying still a lot time - not unlike a certain

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:23.170
bearded dragon that just lost its owner.

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:28.009
They also don’t seem to require much in
the way of food, extracting what little nutrition

00:16:28.009 --> 00:16:32.180
they do need by drawing in household dust
– a lot of the organic matter in dust is

00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:37.240
comprised of dead and discarded human hair
and skin cells, so this makes sense.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:40.630
This feeding is all done through filtered
mouths in the fibers.

00:16:40.630 --> 00:16:47.460
However, this changes if an SCP-799 blanket
is forced to go a long time without food.

00:16:47.460 --> 00:16:53.600
While they possess this biological trait themselves,
SCP-799 don’t seem to regard cold-blooded

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:55.960
animals to be sources of food.

00:16:55.960 --> 00:17:02.269
In fact, they don’t even seem able to detect
other creatures with cold blood, such as reptiles.

00:17:02.269 --> 00:17:08.710
Instead, SCP-799 will metamorphose into a
more predatory form in order to consume any

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:14.390
warm-blooded mammal or human being it encounters,
transforming its feeding orifices and digestive

00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:19.820
tract into a singular mouth lined with several
rows of sharp, pointed teeth.

00:17:19.820 --> 00:17:24.970
From there, it will wrap up the largest, warm-blooded
animal it can find – whether that be a hamster

00:17:24.970 --> 00:17:29.800
or a grown man – and will tear off parts
of its prey, reducing them to little more

00:17:29.800 --> 00:17:34.020
than a thin slurry as it digests their body
mass to feed itself.

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:38.330
So, if you move into a new house and come
across an old, knitted blanket…

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:42.250
maybe consider throwing it out… if you want
to live.

00:17:42.250 --> 00:17:48.060
Now go and check out “SCP-3032 AND THEN
IT WAS AN ANGRY SPIDER” for more warnings

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:52.630
about everyday objects that can seem harmless
at first, but will quickly turn deadly when

00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:54.150
you least expect it.

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:57.430
And after that, if you’re in the market
for a piece of anomalous furniture that’s

00:17:57.430 --> 00:18:03.560
still soft and cosy like the blanket, but
nowhere near as dangerous, then “SCP-3832

00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:05.530
SURPRISE PILLOW FIGHT” might be just what
you need!

